              "text": "Understanding your pregnancy test results depends on whether you took a home urine test or had a quantitative blood test processed at a laboratory. \n\n---\n\n### 1. Home Pregnancy Tests (Urine)\nUrine tests detect **human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG)**, a hormone your body begins producing after a fertilized egg implants in the uterus. \n\n*   **The Control Line (Test Validity):** Every home test has a \"control\" window or line (often marked with a 'C'). **This line must appear for the test to be valid.** If no control line appears, the test is defective, and you must discard it and try another one.\n*   **Positive Result:** A positive result typically displays as two lines, a plus sign, or the word \"Pregnant\" on a digital screen. **Any line in the test zone\u2014no matter how faint\u2014indicates a positive result**, provided it has the appropriate color (pink or blue) and appeared within the instruction manual's specified reading window (usually 3 to 10 minutes).\n*   **Faint Line vs. Evaporation Line:**\n    *   **Faint Positive:** This line has color (pink or blue) and appears within the designated reading time. It usually means you are in the very early stages of pregnancy when hCG levels are still low, or your urine was diluted. \n    *   **Evaporation Line:** This is a faint, colorless, grayish shadow that appears *after* the test\u2019s reading time window has passed. It is caused by urine evaporating off the test strip and does **not** indicate pregnancy.\n*   **Negative Result:** Only the control line is visible, and the test window remains completely blank.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Blood Pregnancy Tests (hCG Levels)\nIf your doctor ordered a quantitative blood test (often called a Beta hCG), it measures the exact amount of the hormone in your blood, reported in milli-international units per milliliter (mIU/mL):\n\n*   **Negative:** An hCG level of **less than 5 mIU/mL** is considered negative for pregnancy.\n*   **Borderline (Grey Area):** An hCG level **between 5 and 25 mIU/mL** is inconclusive. If your result falls in this range, your healthcare provider will likely ask you to repeat the blood test in 48 hours. In a healthy, developing early pregnancy, hCG levels typically double roughly every two days. \n*   **Positive:** An hCG level **greater than 25 mIU/mL** is considered a positive result.\n\n---\n\n### Recommended Next Steps\n\n*   **If your test is positive:** Contact an OB/GYN or midwife to schedule your first prenatal appointment. They will help you establish a timeline, confirm the pregnancy (often via ultrasound), and discuss early pregnancy wellness.\n*   **If your test is negative, but your period is late:** Wait 3 to 5 days and test again. If you test too early, hCG levels may simply be too low to detect. For the most accurate home test, use your **first-morning urine**, which is the most concentrated.\n*   **If you are unsure or get conflicting results:** Schedule an appointment with your healthcare provider. They can perform an in-office urine or blood test to give you a definitive answer.",
